Reads the base gateway JSON (listener, issuer, session key; services optional),
adds one service for every MCPServer with auth.mode header and an
auth.credentialGateway block, and installs the result like "apply". The headers
a server declares in auth.headers become the sign-in inputs, so pushing the
server's .mcp/servers.yaml and running sync configures the gateway and MCP Auth.
Each derived service reads its resource-client key from client.pem, supplied with
--secret-file client.pem=<file>. Header values people submit live only in the
gateway's memory, so restarting MCP Auth does not clear them; restarting the
gateway does.

Usage:
  mcp-runtime cluster mcp-auth-gateway sync [flags]

Flags:
      --context string             Explicit Kubernetes context
      --dry-run                    Validate inputs and show the plan without writing
      --expect-cluster-id string   Expected kube-system namespace UID
      --file string                Gateway JSON file; file references must use /etc/mcp-credential-gateway/<key>
      --gateway-image string       Credential gateway image; defaults to the installed gateway's image on updates
  -h, --help                       help for sync
      --kubeconfig string          Explicit kubeconfig path
      --mcp-host string            Public MCP host for servers that set no ingressHost
      --secret-file stringArray    Mount a private KEY=FILE input (the session binding key and the resource-client key)
      --tls-secret string          Existing TLS Secret covering the auth hostname; no certificate is issued
      --wait-timeout duration      How long to wait for the gateway to become ready before wiring mcp-auth to it (0 skips the wait) (default 3m0s)
      --yes                        Apply the reviewed configuration without another prompt

Global Flags:
      --debug   Enable debug mode with structured error logging
